                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_concentrate:   `<strong>That is the strategic answer.</strong> You cannot defend home, build the future, and internationalise at once on COP$4.200 millones when the projects need COP$7.600 millones. Concentration — fund the few that win their race and kill or defer the rest — is the discipline the award and patents tempt you to abandon.`,
                    coach_r1_keepoptions:   `<strong>Keeping every option open is the trap.</strong> Spread COP$4.200 millones across four projects that need COP$7.600 millones and every one is underfunded and slow — the next-gen sensor ships after the Brazilians land and the EU misses its window. Which two deserved full funding, and which did you owe the discipline of killing?`,
                    coach_r1_euonly:        `<strong>All-in on the EU while home is undefended?</strong> The recognition opened the door, but the Brazilian rival can take 15–20% of your domestic customers and undercut the recurring revenue that funds R&D. Can the EU revenue arrive fast enough to replace the base you are losing?`,
                    coach_r1_defendonly:    `<strong>Pure defence forfeits the EU optionality.</strong> Out-spending the Brazilian rival may hold home, but you walk away from a CE-gated EU window that the award just opened — and you still have not framed the trade-off as concentrate-and-choose.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You chose to concentrate AND named the threat as real — 15–20% of share gone in two quarters as the cheaper rival lands in ~5 months. That is the lens that lets you fund the races you can actually win.`,
                    coach_r1_slow:          `<strong>"Farmers are loyal" is how you lose the recurring base.</strong> The venture-backed rival is buying share with a cheaper sensor; loyalty erodes fast when the value segment defects. Treat the 5-month clock as real, not years away.`,
                    coach_r1_none:          `<strong>Patents and an award do not stop a price-led land-grab.</strong> "No real threat" is exactly the complacency a venture-backed entrant exploits. The threat is real and fast — diagnose it honestly.`,
                    coach_r1_lowhonesty:    `<strong>Low discipline to choose means you will chase everything.</strong> Unless the team accepts that all three fronts at once is infeasible, you will peanut-butter the budget and lose every race. Name the constraint honestly.`,
                    coach_r2_peanut:        `<strong>You peanut-buttered COP$4.200 millones across four projects.</strong> Every one is now underfunded and slow — none ships on its clock. You kept every option open and lost all four races. Concentration was the whole point.`,
                    coach_r2_concentrated:  `<strong>That is portfolio concentration.</strong> By funding the few projects that clear their ship threshold, you get products on the clock instead of four half-built bets. Return-per-peso comes from concentration, not coverage.`,
                    coach_r2_thin:          `<strong>Only one bet actually ships.</strong> Spreading the budget left too many projects below their threshold. Concentrate harder — a funded project that ships beats two that arrive late.`,
                    coach_r2_focus:         `<strong>Funded the winners and freed the team.</strong> Pausing the low-value legacy projects concentrates R&D capacity on the bets that win their races — exactly the fund/defer/kill discipline.`,
                    coach_r2_recurring:     `<strong>You paused the cash engine.</strong> The recurring platform is 60% of revenue and funds R&D — starving it to free cash undermines the home defence and the ARPU you are trying to lift. Pause low-value legacy work instead.`,
                    coach_r3_distributor:   `<strong>The distributor is the disciplined first step.</strong> Fast, low cost, low capital — it opens the EU without spending the budget twice or betting cash you do not have. You trade margin and control for speed and solvency, which is the right trade for a cash-fragile SME.`,
                    coach_r3_jv:            `<strong>A joint venture shares the cost and risk.</strong> Medium control without the subsidiary's capital bill — workable if the partner truly commits. Reconcile it with the R&D envelope: CE-certification still comes from the same COP$4.200 millones.`,
                    coach_r3_subsidiary:    `<strong>A wholly-owned subsidiary costs ~COP$5.000M you do not have.</strong> With COP$2.400 millones in the bank you would be insolvent before the first EU sale. High control and margin are not worth the cash and speed you sacrifice here — a distributor is the disciplined entry.`,
                    coach_r3_skip:          `<strong>Skipping the EU protects cash but forfeits the optionality.</strong> The CE-gated window the award opened will not stay open. Defending home is right, but a low-commitment distributor pilot could have kept the EU option alive cheaply.`,
                    coach_r3_disciplined:   `<strong>Phased distributor entry is textbook real-options.</strong> Secure home first, run a small EU pilot behind a gate, then scale only if it clears. Low commitment buys information and limits downside for a cash-fragile SME.`,
                    coach_r3_now:           `<strong>Full commitment "now" strains the cash you cannot spare.</strong> Pouring resources into the EU while the Brazilian rival attacks home risks the floor collapsing while you reach abroad. Phase it behind a gate.`,
                    coach_r3_defer:         `<strong>Deferring the EU entirely lets the window close.</strong> Securing home matters, but a cheap distributor pilot keeps the option alive without the cash of a full launch. Total deferral forfeits what the award opened.`,
                    coach_r3_nogate:        `<strong>EU spend with no go/no-go gate is uncapped risk.</strong> Without tying spend to CE achieved and a first EU order, a slipping certification drains cash with nothing to show. Stage the commitment behind clear criteria.`,
                    coach_r4_balanced:      `<strong>Balanced sequencing respects both clocks.</strong> Defend home on the 5-month clock and certify for the 9-month EU window — that beats chasing technical elegance that ships at 14 months, after the Brazilian product is already on every shelf.`,
                    coach_r4_clockfirst:    `<strong>Clock-first protects home but underweights the EU.</strong> Shipping "good enough, fast" beats the rival at home — right against the 5-month clock — but make sure CE-certification still lands inside the 9-month window.`,
                    coach_r4_quality:       `<strong>Optimising for elegance ignores the clock.</strong> The most sophisticated build at 14 months arrives nine months after the Brazilian product is on every shelf. A worse product shipped in five months would have held your share — time-to-market is a value driver, not a constraint.`,
                    coach_r4_cuthome:       `<strong>Cutting home first protects the wrong thing.</strong> The home recurring base is the cash engine that funds R&D and the EU move. Cut EU certification first if R&D slips — protect the floor that pays for everything else.`,
                    coach_r4_nocont:        `<strong>No contingency is a bet you cannot afford.</strong> The spend is front-loaded and revenue lags two quarters; if R&D slips you have nothing to cut and the runway runs dry. Name what gets cut first.`,
                    coach_r4_noreserve:     `<strong>No reserve and the runway runs dry mid-build.</strong> Front-loaded R&D plus certification with revenue lagging two quarters leaves you unable to make payroll before anything ships. A brilliant plan with no runway is a bankruptcy plan.`,
                    coach_r4_hoard:         `<strong>Hoarding too much cash starves the plan.</strong> A reserve is prudent, but holding back most of the cash means the funded projects cannot actually ship on the clock. Balance safety against execution.`,

